The bare `catch {}` in `setupWatchers` silently swallows every thrown value, not just the expected 'not a git repository' cases. A programming error inside the try block — e.g., a `TypeError` from `watchFile`, an unexpected exception from `configureBranchWatcher`, or any other unforeseen bug — would be completely invisible. At minimum, unexpected errors should be logged so they surface during debugging:
```typescript
} catch (error) {
  if (!(error instanceof ChangeDetectionUnavailableError)) {
    logger.debug('Change detection git watcher setup failed:', error);
  }
  // Watching is opportunistic; scanning still runs from module graph updates.
}
```

`getGitDir()` falls back to `return gitPath` when `.git` is a file whose content does not contain a recognizable `gitdir:` pointer. In that case `gitPath` is a FILE path (`.git` itself), not a directory. Callers pass the returned value directly to `watchFile(generation, 'head', gitDir, ...)`, which then calls `fs.watch(gitPath, ...)` on a file rather than on the directory that contains `HEAD` and `packed-refs`. The watchers are silently set up on the wrong target and will never fire the events they're meant to detect.

A malformed `.git` pointer is an unusual but theoretically possible state (e.g., a corrupted worktree). The method should throw, log, or at minimum NOT return a path that isn't a directory:
```typescript
if (gitStat.isFile()) {
  const gitPointer = await this.fileSystem.readFile(gitPath, 'utf8');
  const match = /^gitdir:\s+(.+)$/m.exec(gitPointer.trim());
  if (match) {
    return resolvePath(repoRoot, match[1]);
  }
  // Malformed gitdir pointer — fall through to throw or return gitPath's parent
}
```

`gitDiffProvider` is no longer `readonly`. The field is effectively write-once (initialized in the constructor or lazily via `getGitDiffProvider()`), but without `readonly` there is no compiler-enforced guarantee. Any code added later in the class could unintentionally reassign it. Since the lazy-initialization pattern uses `??=` rather than `=`, one alternative is to keep `readonly` and initialize the field in the constructor unconditionally:
```typescript
private readonly gitDiffProvider: GitDiffProvider;
// in constructor:
this.gitDiffProvider = options.gitDiffProvider ?? new GitDiffProvider(this.workingDir);
```
The downside is that `GitDiffProvider` is always constructed even when `enabled = false`, which is the original motivation for the change. If avoiding eager construction matters, the field could be typed as `GitDiffProvider | undefined` but narrowed inside a `readonly`-guarded accessor.
